Advance system time past a zero-debt GXFIFO stall so it cannot deadlock.

WHY. The single-instance host intermittently HARD-freezes on
wireless bring-up, cdb caught spinning in melonDS::GPU3D::CmdFIFORead via
scheduler_run_round from the frontend's present loop. The mechanism is a
scheduler/geometry-engine deadlock, not a radio bug:

  - When the ARM9 writes to a full GX command FIFO, CmdFIFOWrite pushes the
    overflow onto CmdStallQueue and calls NDS.GXFIFOStall() - the ARM9 halts and
    retires nothing until the stall clears.
  - The stall is cleared in exactly one place, CmdFIFORead -> GXFIFOUnstall, and
    CmdFIFORead runs only from ExecuteCommand, which runs only from GPU3D::Run()'s
    drain loop.
  - GPU3D::Run() early-returns WITHOUT draining when a FlushRequest is pending (a
    SwapBuffers awaiting VBlank) or geometry is disabled. So the FIFO stays full,
    the stall stays set, and the ARM9 stays halted.
  - The VBlank that would clear FlushRequest is delivered by nds_tick_display,
    which this scheduler drives off the ARM9 rendezvous timestamp. That timestamp
    only advances by the geometry engine's pending cycle debt while stalled - and
    with Run() refusing to drain, the debt is zero. Timestamp frozen -> no VBlank
    -> FlushRequest never clears -> stall never clears -> timestamp frozen. A
    two-way wait, intermittent because it depends on the exact frame at which the
    FIFO fills relative to the swap.

melonDS does not deadlock here because its SysTimestamp marches to the next
scheduled event during a GX stall (RunSystem keeps running system events while
the ARM9 is CPUStop_GXStall'd); our debt-only advance froze time whenever the
debt was zero.

FIX. In the GXFIFO-stall branch, keep the melonDS-faithful debt-based advance
whenever the geometry engine has real pending work (debt > 0), but when the debt
is zero while still stalled, step the ARM9 timestamp to the planned rendezvous
(arm9_target, the same ~kIterCap step the non-stalled path would take). The ARM9
still retires no instructions - it is stalled - so this only unblocks the device
clocks below (display/SPU/timer), letting the VBlank that clears FlushRequest be
delivered so the FIFO can drain and the stall clear on a following round. The
frontend's own non-advance watchdog remains as a last-resort safety net.

--- a/runner/src/scheduler.cpp
+++ b/runner/src/scheduler.cpp
@@ -327,7 +327,24 @@
             const uint64_t debt =
                 static_cast<uint64_t>(nds_gpu3d_cycles_to_run())
                 << kArm9ClockShift;
-            g_slot[0].cycles = std::min(arm9_target, g_slot[0].cycles + debt);
+            if (debt > 0) {
+                g_slot[0].cycles = std::min(arm9_target, g_slot[0].cycles + debt);
+            } else {
+                // ZERO DEBT WHILE STILL STALLED - break the wedge. The geometry
+                // engine's Run() is refusing to drain the FIFO: it early-returns
+                // on a pending FlushRequest (a SwapBuffers awaiting VBlank) or
+                // with geometry disabled, so no GX cycles are pending, yet the
+                // FIFO stays full and the ARM9 stays GXFIFO-halted. Advancing
+                // only by `debt` would freeze g_slot[0].cycles here, and the
+                // VBlank that clears FlushRequest is driven off THIS timestamp:
+                // the two wait on each other forever (the single-instance
+                // host GPU3D-FIFO wedge). Step system time to the
+                // planned rendezvous so the display/SPU/timer ticks below run and
+                // deliver the event that releases the stall. The ARM9 still
+                // retires nothing - it is stalled - matching melonDS, whose
+                // SysTimestamp marches to the next event during a GX stall.
+                g_slot[0].cycles = arm9_target;
+            }
         } else {
             run_slice(0, static_cast<uint32_t>(arm9_target - g_slot[0].cycles));
         }
